      "source": "https://www.montevideomn.org/?SEC=3C2922D7-C051-418A-A05C-F495CFAEB7C1",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Montevideo sits in a double river valley where the Minnesota and Chippewa rivers converge, about 140 miles west of Minneapolis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montevideo%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The area around Montevideo was populated by Native Americans and fur traders during the first half of the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montevideo%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the Dakota War of 1862, the U.S. government opened the area to homesteaders, leading to rapid settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montevideo%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Railroads were built, and settlers followed, including Civil War veterans, Norwegians, Germans, Swedes, Dutch, and Irish.",
